  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale:\n- Central Idea: We will develop a multi-resolution optimization approach combined with specialized gradient-based methods to improve the upper bound for the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem. The key insight is that the current implementation, while comprehensive, might get stuck in local minima when operating at fixed resolution levels. Our approach will dynamically adjust resolution levels while focusing optimization on problem-critical regions.\n- Why it could improve: By using gradient information to guide searches and progressively refining solutions from coarse to finer grids, we can escape shallow local minima that often trap optimization routines. The method combines three complementary components: (1) smooth approximations of the objective function to enable gradient-based optimization, (2) multi-resolution explores to identify promising regions, and (3) targeted gradient-based searches in these regions.\n- Bottleneck addressed: The primary limitation addressed is the susceptibility to local optima at fixed resolution levels. We also tackle computational efficiency and reproducibility issues common in non-convex optimization.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification:\n- Representation: We use $n$-point samples over $[0,2]$ with $\\Delta x = 2/n$.\n- Variables: $h = (h_0, h_1, \\dots, h_{n-1}) \\in [0,1]^n$\n- Constraints: $\\sum_{i=0}^{n-1} h_i \\Delta x = 1$ (with $\\Delta x = 2/n$)\n- Objective: $C_5 = \\max_{k=-n+1}^{n-1} \\sum_{i=0}^{n-1} h_i (1 - h_{(i+k)_p}) \\Delta x$\n- Smoothed objective: $f_\\alpha(h) = \\frac{1}{\\alpha} \\log(\\sum_{k} \\exp(\\alpha(c_k - m)))$ where $c_k$ is each correlation score, $m = \\max_k c_k$.\n- Gradient: $\\nabla f_\\alpha(h) = \\frac{1}{\\alpha} \\sum_{k} \\frac{w_k}{\\sum_j w_j} [\\operatorname{clip}(1-h_{(i+k)_p}, 0, 1) - \\operatorname{clip}(-h_{(i+k)_p}, 0, 0)] \\Delta x$ (simplified form)\n- Feasible set projection: $h^\\text{proj} = \\mathbb{P}_{[0,1]^n}(\\hat{h}) + t \\mathbf{1}$ where $t$ adjusts sum to meet constraint.\n- Key mathematical innovations: \n  - Smoothed objective preserves max operation but allows gradient-based optimization\n  - Multi-resolution strategy exploits different feature scales \n  - Gradient guidance focuses search effort away from poor-performing regions\n\n3. Implementation Procedure:\n- SmallProjectStep: Adam optimizer with learning rate 0.5 with projection\n- LargeProjectStep: Regularize problem with $\\ell_1$ penalty (weight 0.1) and use SCS for convex optimization\n- EmbeddingStrategy: Random embedding into 4-dimensional space optimizing resolution encoding\n- Tunable parameters: $\\alpha \\in \\{50000,500000\\}$ (temperature), $\\mu \\in \\{0, 0.001\\}$ (regularization coefficient), $\\delta_\\text{step} \\in \\{0.001, 0.005\\}$ (optimizer step size)\n\n4. Search Initialization:\n- Start with provided initial_h_values if available (n=256 samples)\n- If no initial values, use uniform grid with points randomly sampled from [0,1] and adjust to meet sum constraint\n- Always begin optimization from projected initial point\n- Primary optimization switches:\n  - Step1: Multi-resolution optimization for 10 minutes\n  - Step2: Gradient projection attempt\n  - Step3: Top-shift focus optimization\n  - Step4: Cut-plane method for embedded problem\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness:\n- Minimum time requirement: 300 seconds per resolution level\n- Multi-resolution strategy: Start at 256 points, then 512, then 1024\n- Time allocation: 50% for adaptive searches, 30% for top-shift optimization, 20% for validation/restart\n- Feasibility checks: At each step, clip values to [0,1] and adjust to meet sum constraint\n- Robustness handling:\n  - Upon NaN/Inf in gradient, use derivative-free optimization\n  - If correlation scores saturated, add damping factor\n  - Solution falls below 0.38 constraint, retain but monitor\n- Convergence criteria: No improvement seen for >600 seconds, or function value stabilizes under tolerance 1e-8\n- Valid fallback: Perform coarse grid optimization as last resort\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract:\n- Final candidate passes: 0<=h[i]<=1 $\\forall i$, $\\sum h_i \\Delta x = 1$, and $C_5 \\leq 0.38$\n- During evaluation: Pre-check sum constraint and make sure projection preserves original solution quality\n- Objective revalidation: Recompute $C_5$ using original correlation method\n- Final output format: (h, final_c5, n_points), where n_points is always even and between 256 and 2048\n- Progress logging: Summary of best value seen at levels: 256, 512, 1024 points\n- Metrics to record: Mean and standard deviation of correlations across all lags, not just the max",
